WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.540
Although they’re often electric,&nbsp;
on-screen kisses aren’t always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:09.060
planned. From the tender to the funny to the&nbsp;
deeply controversial, here are a few on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.478
kisses that were never supposed to happen.

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:14.215
One of Lost in Translation’s most iconic scenes 

00:00:14.215 --> 00:00:18.900
revolves around the tender moment shared at the&nbsp;
end of the movie between Scarlett Johansson’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.580
Charlotte and Bill Murray’s character Bob. During&nbsp;
filming, Murray famously whispered something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.124
Johansson's ear that neither actor has revealed.&nbsp;
The ensuing kiss wasn’t planned, either.

00:00:28.124 --> 00:00:36.224
*Bob kisses Charlotte*

00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:39.720
When asked about the scene, director&nbsp;
Sofia Coppola told The Daily Beast,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.900
"I remember sometimes [Murray]&nbsp;
would spring things on [Johansson],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.835
and it was fun to get her reaction."

00:00:44.835 --> 00:00:47.514
During Stranger Things 2’s season finale, 

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:52.320
Lucas and newcomer Max share a kiss at their&nbsp;
school’s winter dance. In an episode of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.120
aftershow series Beyond Stranger Things, Sadie&nbsp;
Sink — who played Max — explained that the kiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.866
was a surprise and that showrunners Matt and&nbsp;
Ross Duffer sprang it on her the day of filming.

00:01:01.866 --> 00:01:05.340
"Oh, Sadie, you ready for the kiss?"
"I’m like, what? No! No, that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.320
in the script. You can’t, that’s not happening."

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.180
Ross Duffer had previously joked with Sink&nbsp;
about her kissing her co-star Caleb McLaughlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.551
and the idea had stressed her out so much&nbsp;
that he decided to have it happen for real.

00:01:16.551 --> 00:01:20.520
It was a decision which sparked outrage&nbsp;
online. In the wake of the controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.291
Sink told TheWrap that she had “always&nbsp;
felt comfortable” on set, saying

00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:26.520
“[The Duffer Brothers] always create a comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.880
space. And if I felt uncomfortable with&nbsp;
anything, I wouldn’t have done it.”

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.820
"Anyways, it's great. I can’t&nbsp;
imagine it without it. Thank you."&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.678
"Yeah. You're welcome."

00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:36.631
Luke and Leia’s scene towards 

00:01:36.631 --> 00:01:40.200
the end of The Last Jedi is arguably&nbsp;
one of the movie’s finest moments.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.560
It’s an exchange which carries&nbsp;
extra weight considering Carrie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.640
Fisher’s tragic passing only months after&nbsp;
filming ended. The scene is punctuated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.480
a forehead kiss from Luke to Leia which&nbsp;
was improvised on Mark Hamill’s part.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:56.700
"It was just spontaneous, and really momentous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.463
for me because Luke was saying&nbsp;
goodbye to his sister forever."

00:02:00.463 --> 00:02:03.231
Hamill has been unable to watch the scene since.

00:02:03.231 --> 00:02:07.934
“She’ll be forever missed,&nbsp;
and she’s irreplaceable.”
 &nbsp;

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.360
After the home release of The Hunger Games:&nbsp;
Mockingjay – Part 2, Woody Harrelson told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.100
Entertainment Tonight that his on-screen kiss with&nbsp;
Elizabeth Banks had nothing to do with the script.

00:02:17.279 --> 00:02:20.639
"She didn't know I was even gonna&nbsp;
do it, and I just kissed her."

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:22.978
Harrelson later told the Associated Press,

00:02:22.978 --> 00:02:27.501
“I, personally, always wanted to kiss her. I&nbsp;
don't know if it was the right thing to do."]

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:31.560
Although Harrelson denied knowing it, the&nbsp;
AP’s reporter claimed she'd heard that Banks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.930
intentionally flubbed takes during the scene&nbsp;
so she could keep locking lips with Harrelson.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.880
Thanks to the logistical realities&nbsp;
of filming a television show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.080
an unplanned kiss between two actors can&nbsp;
often involve more people than initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.880
intended. That’s what happened after Game&nbsp;
of Thrones actors Gemma Whelan and Indira&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.723
Varma improvised a kiss between their&nbsp;
characters Yara Greyjoy and Ellaria Sand.

00:02:52.723 --> 00:03:00.249
“Leave him be. Seems like&nbsp;
you don’t need a protector.”

00:03:00.875 --> 00:03:07.440
This moment takes place on a ship just prior to&nbsp;
an attack by antagonist Euron Greyjoy. Whelan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.860
back was injured at the time, however, and&nbsp;
since she couldn't perform all of her stunts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.841
her double was brought in to replace&nbsp;
her — and also had to kiss Varma.

00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:16.881
Varma told Entertainment Weekly that

00:03:16.881 --> 00:03:21.060
“I had to start kissing this poor stunt&nbsp;
double and she was so terrified. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.720
think she'd ever been put in that situation&nbsp;
before. She's used to falling over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.920
being attacked and all the stunts, but to be&nbsp;
kissed by an actress was a bit beyond her."

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.960
According to Bryce Dallas Howard, the&nbsp;
kiss between her character Claire and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.495
Chris Pratt’s Owen was never in the script&nbsp;
for Jurassic World. She told Out Magazine,

00:03:38.495 --> 00:03:41.160
"We had shot the scene in the way&nbsp;
that it was written — there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.734
kiss. And then on the last day, that&nbsp;
happened — what you see in the movie."

00:03:44.734 --> 00:03:48.960
The movie’s director, Colin Trevorrow,&nbsp;
however, has since revealed that it wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.740
entirely spontaneous. He took Pratt&nbsp;
aside and suggested he kiss Howard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.571
but never told her. On Jurassic&nbsp;
World’s commentary, he explained:

00:03:56.571 --> 00:04:00.840
"The idea was we're just going to have Chris&nbsp;
Pratt surprise [Howard] in front of 200 people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.101
and that's the take that's in the movie."

00:04:03.101 --> 00:04:06.134
During the season 3 premiere of The Office, 

00:04:06.134 --> 00:04:10.380
Steve Carell’s character Michael learns&nbsp;
that his employee Oscar — played by Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.558
Nuñez — is gay. In a misguided attempt to&nbsp;
prove how tolerant he is… this happens.

00:04:20.836 --> 00:04:21.960
*Michael kisses Oscar*

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:29.518
According to Nuñez, however, the script only&nbsp;
called for Carell to hug him. He told the AV Club,

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:33.240
"We were just supposed to hug, and he&nbsp;
kept hugging me. And that particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.639
take he came in really close, and I'm&nbsp;
like, 'Where is he going with this?'"

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:38.779
”Thank you.”

00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:39.360
”Thank you.”

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:39.960
”See?”

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.340
David O. Russell didn't cast Jennifer Lawrence in&nbsp;
his 2013 black comedy American Hustle until very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.020
late into the casting process. The director told&nbsp;
Vanity Fair that even though she was young for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.100
the role, Lawrence had a, quote, "timeless&nbsp;
quality," and that "she took to the part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.360
chemically." Since we’re talking about&nbsp;
an improvised kiss scene between Lawrence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.134
and Amy Adams, it would be interesting to learn&nbsp;
exactly which chemicals Russell was talking about.

00:05:05.134 --> 00:05:09.600
Lawrence played Rosalyn Rosenfeld,&nbsp;
wife to conman Irving Rosenfeld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.831
played by Christian Bale. At&nbsp;
a pivotal moment in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.831 --> 00:05:18.540
Rosalyn argues with Irving's mistress Sydney,&nbsp;
played by Amy Adams, in a bathroom, and in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:23.820
middle of the confrontation Rosalyn aggressively&nbsp;
kisses Sydney and then bursts into cruel laughter.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.500
Russell said the kiss was improvised, recalling&nbsp;
that the idea was pitched to him, but that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.080
didn’t exactly fit his vision. In a 2013 press&nbsp;
conference about the movie, Adams said even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.650
the kiss was her idea, the credit for the moment&nbsp;
goes to Lawrence. Adams went on to say the kiss:

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.360
"[...] didn't just feel like&nbsp;
a moment where two girls are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.580
going to kiss onscreen it felt emotional,"

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.910
and called Lawrence's laugh&nbsp;
that followed the kiss "genius."

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.822
*laughter*

00:05:52.822 --> 00:05:57.960
In 2014's Edge of Tomorrow, United&nbsp;
Defense Force Major William Cage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:03.240
played by Tom Cruise, is arrested by a superior&nbsp;
officer for insubordination during an alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.500
invasion. After his records are deliberately&nbsp;
altered to make it look like he's a private,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.060
Cage is forced into action, where he quickly&nbsp;
dies in battle only to discover he's trapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.560
in a time loop where he relives the same&nbsp;
day leading up to his death over and over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.900
His only ally is Rita Vrataski, played by&nbsp;
Emily Blunt, a highly regarded sergeant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.165
who's also the only person who accepts&nbsp;
that his time-looping dilemma is real.

00:06:26.165 --> 00:06:29.574
“Find me when you wake up.”

00:06:29.574 --> 00:06:34.140
Speaking to Film School Rejects, screenwriter&nbsp;
Christopher McQuarrie said the filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.260
struggled to find a place in the&nbsp;
film for Cage and Rita to kiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.020
but nothing they tried seemed genuine. He said:

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.580
"[I]t always felt false. It always felt like they&nbsp;
weren't focused on what was really important."

00:06:45.201 --> 00:06:49.020
McQuarrie and his colleagues gave up on&nbsp;
the kiss completely, but apparently Blunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:53.280
hadn't. McQuarrie said that as they shot a&nbsp;
scene in which Rita says goodbye to Cage:

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.620
"[Blunt] just kissed him goodbye in the moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:58.301
And it was not in the script.&nbsp;
It was not even discussed."

00:06:58.301 --> 00:07:01.200
According to McQuarrie after the&nbsp;
scene was filmed, Blunt said:

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.080
"It just felt right. It felt right and I did it."

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.780
In season 4 of Riverdale, CW's teen drama&nbsp;
paid tribute to Hedwig and the Angry Inch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.480
a stage musical later adapted to film about an&nbsp;
East German trans rock singer in the '80s. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.940
the episode "Chapter Seventy-Four: Wicked Little&nbsp;
Town," Casey Cott’s Kevin Keller decides it's time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.800
for Riverdale High to host variety shows again.&nbsp;
When Principal Honey warns Kevin not to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.780
any numbers from Hedwig, Kevin and his friends&nbsp;
fight back by performing multiple numbers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:36.540
the musical. During the Hedwig original "Tear&nbsp;
Me Down" Kevin and K.J. Apa’s Archie lock lips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.860
and according to Cott even though the kiss was&nbsp;
never planned, it absolutely had to happen.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.660
Cott explained to TV Insider that there's&nbsp;
a part of the Hedwig stage musical when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.400
the titular hero goes into the audience and&nbsp;
kisses an audience member. Inspired by this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.900
while filming his performance of "Tear Me&nbsp;
Down" with Cott's Kevin Keller playing Hedwig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.320
Cott kissed Apa on the cheek.&nbsp;
But then, as Cott explained:

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.740
"[...] the next take I went in to&nbsp;
do the same thing and [Apa] turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.280
his face and we just kind of laid a big&nbsp;
one on each other. And then after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.660
we were like 'This is exactly what it should&nbsp;
be. This is, like, perfect for this number.'"

00:08:09.868 --> 00:08:12.808
Thankfully, everyone agreed,&nbsp;
and the kiss made the final cut.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.440
From the beginning of the sci-fi&nbsp;
crime drama Person of Interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:21.600
fans noticed romantic tension between&nbsp;
Jim Caviezel’s CIA operative, John Reese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:27.180
and NYPD Detective Joss Carter, played&nbsp;
by Taraji P. Henson. However, it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.140
until the third season for those feelings&nbsp;
to find any kind of physical expression.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:36.420
In season 3's "The Crossing," Reese and Carter&nbsp;
finally share a kiss while they're hiding from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:41.580
bad guys in a morgue; sadly, it's destined to be&nbsp;
their first and last. At the end of the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:46.560
the corrupt cop Patrick Simmons ambushes them&nbsp;
and shoots them both before running away.&nbsp;

00:08:46.935 --> 00:08:51.435
They're both hit, but Carter is mortally&nbsp;
wounded and she dies in Reese's arms.

00:08:51.435 --> 00:08:55.620
According to the show's producers, even though&nbsp;
the kiss was a landmark moment for the characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.400
it was a decision the actors made&nbsp;
completely on their own. Person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.910
of Interest executive producer Greg&nbsp;
Plageman told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:07.200
"[...] the kiss was not scripted. We never&nbsp;
wrote it in [...] It wasn't in every take;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:10.534
there were takes where they didn't do&nbsp;
it and there were takes where they did."

00:09:10.534 --> 00:09:14.400
Plageman went on to say that when they&nbsp;
looked at the takes without the kiss, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.060
felt like something was missing and that the kiss&nbsp;
deepened the connection between the characters.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.500
Sometimes, an unplanned kiss in a&nbsp;
TV show or a movie will generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.720
controversy felt well outside the editing room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.000
and that's something Tommy Davidson learned&nbsp;
while filming the 1998 romantic comedy Woo.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.880
Jada Pinkett Smith played Davidson's love interest&nbsp;
in the romcom, and in his 2020 memoir Living in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.740
Color: What's Funny About Me, Davidson writes&nbsp;
that neither he nor Smith were satisfied with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.640
the script's ending. He says he pitched the&nbsp;
idea of ending Woo with the couple dancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.720
in the middle of a city street, and apparently&nbsp;
everyone liked the idea. While dancing with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.520
co-star in the new scene, Davidson kissed Smith&nbsp;
without warning. Though he said Smith flinched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.420
when he kissed her, at the time he, quote,&nbsp;
"didn't think it was all that big of a deal."

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.920
But there was someone who thought it was&nbsp;
a big deal Jada Pinkett Smith's husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.960
Will Smith. According to Davidson, after&nbsp;
the scene was shot, Will Smith followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.640
Davidson into his trailer and things got very&nbsp;
heated between the two. Davidson explained:

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.180
"There was a moment there when&nbsp;
we might have come to blows. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.780
reactive fight-or-flight instinct was&nbsp;
triggered and I could have exploded."

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:22.440
Thankfully, Jada stepped in and&nbsp;
helped to calm things down, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.020
Davidson and Will remained good friends afterward.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.819
“I was just about to retaliate!”

00:10:27.819 --> 00:10:31.980
Between the '60s and '80s, Valerie Leon made&nbsp;
a name for herself playing young beauties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:38.760
particularly in James Bond flicks she appeared&nbsp;
in both 1977's The Spy Who Loved Me and 1983's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:45.300
Never Say Never Again. In a 2017 interview with&nbsp;
WestEndExtra, Leon named one of the Bond actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:50.100
Roger Moore as one of her favorite actors to&nbsp;
work with. Along with The Spy Who Loved Me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:55.440
she worked with Moore in the TV series The&nbsp;
Saint and the 1978 war film The Wild Geese.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.640
But Leon said that one of her favorite&nbsp;
collaborations with Moore was on the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:05.940
'70s action comedy TV series The Persuaders! She&nbsp;
appears as Space Queen in "The Long Goodbye,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.560
as one of a number of women claiming to be&nbsp;
heiress to a dead scientist. She recalled:

00:11:10.935 --> 00:11:16.095
"Roger gave me an unscripted kiss. I just&nbsp;
closed my eyes and enjoyed it. They kept it in."

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.640
There have been plenty of steamy moments&nbsp;
on the medical drama Grey's Anatomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.980
but in the show's season 14 finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:27.240
there was one that was less steamy but plenty&nbsp;
surprising for almost everyone involved.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:32.040
Early in the episode "All of Me" even though she's&nbsp;
about to get married to Justin Chambers’ Alex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:37.560
and everyone insists they can't see one another&nbsp;
before the wedding Camilla Luddington’s Jo rushes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.520
to see Alex and her other friends, because&nbsp;
she has news she can't keep to herself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.720
She's just learned she's been tapped for&nbsp;
a highly sought-after surgery fellowship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:51.240
at Massachusetts General Hospital. Ignoring&nbsp;
all the protests about not seeing her fiancé,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:57.000
Jo announces it to her fiends, predictably&nbsp;
kisses Alex, and then much less predictably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.200
kisses Jessica Capshaw’s Arizona,&nbsp;
before giving her fiancé a second kiss.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.340
It's just a quick kiss but Capshaw is&nbsp;
visibly surprised, and the moment didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.840
go unnoticed by the audience. Asked about&nbsp;
the kiss on Twitter, Luddington responded:

00:12:10.153 --> 00:12:16.453
"Ha!!! It was unscripted but I thought hello?!!!&nbsp;
Who wouldn't want to also smooch Arizona Robbins."

00:12:16.453 --> 00:12:19.800
Fittingly, you could interpret&nbsp;
it as a kiss goodbye, as "All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.960
Me" was Capshaw's final appearance on the series.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:28.980
The Model Elle Macpherson hasn't done a lot of&nbsp;
acting work, and when she starred in 1997's The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:34.380
Edge, she'd done even less. Macpherson plays&nbsp;
Mickey, wife of billionaire Charles Morse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:40.020
played by Anthony Hopkins. Hopkins and co-star&nbsp;
Alec Baldwin who plays photographer Bob Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:45.540
have the lion's share of screen time. Early&nbsp;
in the film Morse suspects Bob is having an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:50.280
affair with Mickey, and this is confirmed at a&nbsp;
particularly inconvenient time when Morse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.300
Green are trapped in the wilderness and fighting&nbsp;
for their lives against a massive Kodiak bear.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:13:00.240
In a 2011 episode of The Graham Norton Show,&nbsp;
Macpherson said her unexpected kiss with Hopkins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.800
took place during the auditioning process for&nbsp;
The Edge rather than during the filming; and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.900
wasn't so much that the kiss was unexpected,&nbsp;
but the type of kiss was a shock. She said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:14.460
during the audition, the casting directors&nbsp;
emphasized that in spite of the 27-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:19.500
age difference between her and Hopkins it was&nbsp;
